# id  tag  kind  t_s  alt_km  L_si_W  flag  note
1 A ablation_onset 2.2650 80.574 7.5407e+09 stylized  Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~81 km as low-confidence. Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin, volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
2 - first_light 2.4240 79.211 1.1376e+10 info  L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
3 Y first_yield 6.5280 44.159 2.1056e+12 physical  Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 0.5 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5 / H_f in [R22]).
4 B first_breakup 8.5950 26.613 1.2466e+14 stylized  Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification. Light grew x59.2 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
5 F flare 8.5965 26.601 4.7215e+15 stylized  Light rises x41.0 in 0.09 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: split gen 0 at 26.6 km (99% of m0); yield gen 1 at 26.6 km (38% of m0); yield gen 1 at 26.6 km (19% of m0) +2 more. Dust groups supply 100% of the rise and 98% of the light at the end of the flare (the model releases dust all at once, so a dust-dominated first flare is by construction). A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
6 - drag_overtakes_ablation 8.6220 26.392 6.2029e+15 stylized  Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14 km/s (from the model's heating law).
7 F flare 9.1754 21.910 1.3195e+15 stylized  Light rises x2.0 in 0.09 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: split gen 1 at 21.7 km (9% of m0); yield gen 2 at 21.7 km (4% of m0); yield gen 2 at 21.7 km (2% of m0) +7 more. Dust groups supply 79% of the rise and 42% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
8 F flare 9.3635 20.305 1.9688e+15 stylized  Light rises x1.7 in 0.08 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: split gen 1 at 20.2 km (18% of m0); yield gen 2 at 20.2 km (7% of m0); yield gen 2 at 20.2 km (3% of m0) +7 more. Dust groups supply 77% of the rise and 49% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
9 F flare 9.5721 18.540 1.7254e+15 stylized  Light rises x2.1 in 0.13 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: split gen 2 at 19.2 km (1% of m0); yield gen 3 at 19.2 km (0% of m0); yield gen 3 at 19.2 km (0% of m0) +40 more. Dust groups supply 82% of the rise and 49% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
10 P peak 9.5953 18.286 1.1368e+16 physical  Maximum light. It sits ~+0.5 km from the peak of energy deposition (17.8 km) because the luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 13.6 km/s).
11 D rolloff 9.6235 18.102 3.9969e+15 physical  Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 0.03 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used up (speed 88% of v0, 61% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
12 b bump 9.8229 16.619 2.2838e+15 stylized  Small fluctuation, 21% above the local trend; coincides with a split (gen 2) at 16.5 km. The fine structure of this phase is set by the cascade rules, so its pattern is not predictive of a real event.
13 b bump 10.0795 14.753 1.9061e+15 stylized  Small fluctuation, 7% above the local trend; coincides with a split (gen 3) at 15.2 km. The fine structure of this phase is set by the cascade rules, so its pattern is not predictive of a real event.
14 R retirement_step 10.0866 14.713 1.7970e+15 artifact  A group is removed because it slowed below v_dark = 3.0 km/s; the light drops 21% within 0.03 s. A bookkeeping step, not a physical dimming.
15 b bump 10.3741 12.895 1.1451e+15 stylized  Small fluctuation, 36% above the local trend; coincides with a split (gen 3) at 13.2 km. The fine structure of this phase is set by the cascade rules, so its pattern is not predictive of a real event.
16 R retirement_step 10.3747 12.894 1.1243e+15 artifact  A group is removed because it slowed below v_dark = 3.0 km/s; the light drops 40% within 0.03 s. A bookkeeping step, not a physical dimming.
